      <Comment source="pmngroup">There are two genes in Arabidopsis encoding functional threonine aldolase, THA1 and THA2. The two genes have overlapping yet distinct expression patterns. THA1 is primarily expressed in seeds and seedlings, whereas THA2 is expressed in vascular tissues throughout the plant. Knock-out mutants of THA1 have a 50-fold buildup of threonine and a 50% decrease of glycine content in seedlings. Knock-out mutants of THA2 is lethal. The THA2 mutant can be rescued by overproduction of threonine deaminase, another route of threonine degradation, indicating that over-accumulation of threonine rather than the decrease of glycine causes the lethal phenotype. It also indicates that glycine production via the threonine aldolase route is not essential in Arabidopsis.</Comment>
